---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="OpenRouter"
  eyebrow="Providers"
  description="Configure OpenRouter models, DeepSeek Flash snapshots, upstream routing, and service tiers in Roder."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/memories/", title: "Memory & embeddings" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/tools/", title: "Tools & policy" }}
>
  <p>
    Roder exposes OpenRouter as the provider id <code>openrouter</code>. The default
    built-in model route is <code>x-ai/grok-4.6</code>, surfaced in Roder as
    <code>openrouter/x-ai/grok-4.6</code> when selected from provider/model shorthand.
    The route has a 500k context window, auto-compacts near 450k tokens, and advertises
    tool use, structured outputs, image input, and patch-style edits. Compaction
    runs on Roder's client side; this route does not advertise Responses-native
    server compaction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deepseek-flash-1017">DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017</h2>
  <p>
    CLI 0.4.6 and later include the October 17, 2026 DeepSeek Flash snapshot as
    <code>deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017</code>. Select it explicitly; it does
    not replace OpenRouter's default Grok route.
  </p>
  <pre><code>RODER_PROVIDER=openrouter RODER_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017 roder</code></pre>
  <p>
    The catalog declares a 1,048,576-token context window, image input, tool use,
    and structured output. Reasoning defaults to <code>high</code>; supported
    efforts are <code>none</code>, <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, and
    <code>high</code>, not <code>max</code>. Roder uses client-side compaction
    near 943,718 tokens. Actual model access and upstream availability still
    depend on your OpenRouter account and routing choices.
  </p>

  <h2>API key setup</h2>
  <p>
    OpenRouter uses API-key auth. Paste a key through the TUI provider prompt,
    <code>providers/configure</code>, or one of the supported environment variables.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export OPENROUTER_API_KEY="..."
export RODER_PROVIDER=openrouter
export RODER_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.6
cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <pre><code># ~/.roder/config.toml
provider = "openrouter"
model = "x-ai/grok-4.6"

[providers.openrouter]
api_key = "..."
api_key_env = "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"</code></pre>

  <p>
    Roder also checks <code>RODER_OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code>. OpenRouter-compatible
    endpoints can be pointed at another base URL with <code>OPENROUTER_BASE_URL</code>,
    <code>RODER_OPENROUTER_BASE_URL</code>, or <code>providers.openrouter.base_url</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Attribution headers</h2>
  <p>
    Optional OpenRouter attribution headers are configured outside the app-server
    <code>providers/configure</code> API. Roder only sends them when values are present.
  </p>

  <pre><code>[providers.openrouter]
http_referer = "https://example.com"
app_title = "Roder"</code></pre>

  <p>
    Equivalent environment variables are <code>OPENROUTER_HTTP_REFERER</code>,
    <code>RODER_OPENROUTER_HTTP_REFERER</code>, <code>OPENROUTER_APP_TITLE</code>, and
    <code>RODER_OPENROUTER_APP_TITLE</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Upstream routing and service tiers</h2>
  <p>
    OpenRouter can serve a model through several upstream providers. Roder sends
    <code>provider.sort = "price"</code> by default. Configure a routing object to
    replace that default, or use a per-model table to replace the global routing
    object for one model slug.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`[providers.openrouter]
service_tier = "flex"

[providers.openrouter.routing]
sort = "throughput"

[providers.openrouter.model_routing."deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash"]
only = ["deepinfra/fp8"]
allow_fallbacks = false`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    This example prefers throughput globally and pins the named DeepSeek route to
    one upstream without fallback. Routing options are passed as OpenRouter's
    <code>provider</code> object, including <code>only</code>, <code>order</code>,
    <code>allow_fallbacks</code>, <code>sort</code>, and <code>max_price</code>.
    Check upstream availability before disabling fallbacks.
  </p>

  <p>
    To restore OpenRouter's own load balancing instead of Roder's price preference,
    use an empty routing object in place of the routing table:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`[providers.openrouter]
routing = {}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The provider's <code>service_tier</code> setting supports values such as
    <code>flex</code>, <code>priority</code>, and <code>ultrafast</code>. A runtime
    per-request tier takes precedence over this setting. Availability and billing
    depend on the selected model and upstream.
  </p>

  <h2>Model discovery</h2>
  <p>
    Roder returns built-in OpenRouter models immediately, refreshes stale discovery
    in the background with <code>GET /models</code>, and caches successful results in
    <code>~/.roder/models-cache.json</code>. Discovery is keyed by provider id and base URL.
  </p>

  <pre><code>RODER_MODELS_REFRESH=1 cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_MODELS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=0 cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_MODELS_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/roder-models-cache.json cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>Request behavior</h2>
  <p>
    OpenRouter runs through Roder's Responses-compatible transport at
    <code>POST /responses</code>. Requests send the OpenRouter model slug exactly,
    including provider-prefixed ids such as <code>x-ai/grok-4.6</code>.
    Roder does not send OpenAI encrypted reasoning replay fields to OpenRouter by
    default.
  </p>

  <p>
    Requests carry a stable conversation key as <code>prompt_cache_key</code> and
    <code>session_id</code>, using the runtime prompt-cache key or the turn's thread
    id. This lets OpenRouter keep a conversation on the same upstream for prompt
    caching; it does not guarantee a cache hit. The session id is capped at 256
    characters.
  </p>

  <pre><code>RODER_OPENROUTER_LIVE=1 OPENROUTER_LIVE_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.6 cargo test -p roder-ext-openrouter --test live_openrouter -- --ignored</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Provider/model split.</strong> Direct xAI uses model ids such as
    <code>grok-4.6</code>. OpenRouter uses slugs such as
    <code>x-ai/grok-4.6</code>, and Roder preserves that slug.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
